{"@context":{"lean":"lean:","block":"block:","imports":{"@type":"@id"}},"@graph":[{"@id":"lean:module:QOU.Hecke.IdenticalParticleSymmetrization","@type":"LeanModule","name":"QOU.Hecke.IdenticalParticleSymmetrization","imports":["lean:module:Init","lean:module:Mathlib.Tactic.Ring","lean:module:Mathlib.Algebra.Group.Basic"],"declarations":10,"chunks":1},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Hecke.IdenticalParticle.orbit_relabel_invariant","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Hecke.IdenticalParticle.orbit_relabel_invariant","kind":"theorem","line":201,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Hecke/IdenticalParticleSymmetrization.lean#L201-L210","doc":"**Relabeling = conjugation is trace-invariant (the orbit identity).** For any\ncyclic trace `τ` (`τ(xy)=τ(yx)`) on a group, conjugation by `g` preserves it:\n`τ(g a g⁻¹) = τ a`. This is `atom_orbit_reduction`'s `tr_M(β_σ W β_σ⁻¹)=tr_M(W)`.\nIt does **not** give `τ(a g) = τ(a)` — the Young symmetrizer composes `β_σ` rather\nthan conjugating, so that (generally false) equality is exactly the binding signal\n`log(S_free/S_atom)`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Hecke.IdenticalParticle.newton_e4","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Hecke.IdenticalParticle.newton_e4","kind":"theorem","line":179,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Hecke/IdenticalParticleSymmetrization.lean#L179-L187","doc":"**Newton–Frobenius Z=4, fermionic.** `24·e₄ = p₁⁴ − 6p₁²p₂ + 3p₂² + 8p₁p₃ − 6p₄`,\n`e₄ = abcd` (signs `ε_μ`). ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Hecke.IdenticalParticle.newton_h4","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Hecke.IdenticalParticle.newton_h4","kind":"theorem","line":163,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Hecke/IdenticalParticleSymmetrization.lean#L163-L177","doc":"**Newton–Frobenius Z=4, bosonic.** `24·h₄ = p₁⁴ + 6p₁²p₂ + 3p₂² + 8p₁p₃ + 6p₄`\n(`4!/z_μ = 1,6,3,8,6` over `μ = (1⁴),(2,1,1),(2,2),(3,1),(4)`). ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Hecke.IdenticalParticle.newton_e3","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Hecke.IdenticalParticle.newton_e3","kind":"theorem","line":156,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Hecke/IdenticalParticleSymmetrization.lean#L156-L161","doc":"**Newton–Frobenius Z=3, fermionic.** `6·e₃ = p₁³ − 3p₁p₂ + 2p₃`, `e₃ = abc`\n(signs `ε_μ = (−1)^{3−ℓ(μ)}`). ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Hecke.IdenticalParticle.newton_h3","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Hecke.IdenticalParticle.newton_h3","kind":"theorem","line":147,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Hecke/IdenticalParticleSymmetrization.lean#L147-L154","doc":"**Newton–Frobenius Z=3, bosonic.** `6·h₃ = p₁³ + 3p₁p₂ + 2p₃`\n(`z₍₁,₁,₁₎=6, z₍₂,₁₎=2, z₍₃₎=3`; coefficients `3! / z_μ = 1,3,2`). `h₃` is the\ncomplete-homogeneous degree-3 symmetric function in `a,b,c`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Hecke.IdenticalParticle.schur_weyl_Z4","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Hecke.IdenticalParticle.schur_weyl_Z4","kind":"theorem","line":90,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Hecke/IdenticalParticleSymmetrization.lean#L90-L134","doc":"**Z = 4 Schur–Weyl** (⁸Be / first composite Z: pp-pp, nn-nn double shells).\nThe naive distinguishable `p₁⁴` decomposes over the five `S₄` irreps with\nmultiplicities `f^λ = #SYT(λ)`: `f^(4)=1, f^(3,1)=3, f^(2,2)=2, f^(2,1,1)=3,\nf^(1,1,1,1)=1`, so `Σ (f^λ)² = 1+9+4+9+1 = 24 = 4! = |S₄|`. The `(2,2)` sector\nenters with **multiplicity 2** and the Klein `V₄ ◁ S₄` (`S₄/V₄ ≅ S₃`) carries the\ndouble-pair (pp × nn) exchange — recovering the `6 = |S₃| = χ(Bring)` Bring\nsymmetry of Step 4. Each `s_λ` is in the monomial-symmetric basis\n`s_λ = Σ_μ K_{λμ} m_μ` (Kostka numbers `K`); the five groups below are\n`s₍₄₎`, `3·s₍₃,₁₎`, `2·s₍₂,₂₎`, `3·s₍₂,₁,₁₎`, `s₍₁,₁,₁,₁₎`. Sympy- and\nbialternant-verified; `ring`-closed. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Hecke.IdenticalParticle.schur_weyl_Z3","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Hecke.IdenticalParticle.schur_weyl_Z3","kind":"theorem","line":77,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Hecke/IdenticalParticleSymmetrization.lean#L77-L88","doc":"**Z = 3 Schur–Weyl** (Li ppp / nnn). `p₁³` decomposes into the symmetric\n`s₍₃₎ = h₃` (`f^(3)=1`), the standard rep `s₍₂,₁₎` with **multiplicity 2**\n(`f^(2,1)=2`), and the antisymmetric `s₍₁,₁,₁₎ = e₃ = abc` (`f^(1,1,1)=1`). The\n`1,2,1` are the `#SYT` of `S₃`; `Σ (f^λ)² = 1+4+1 = 6 = 3! = |S₃|`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Hecke.IdenticalParticle.two_e2","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Hecke.IdenticalParticle.two_e2","kind":"theorem","line":73,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Hecke/IdenticalParticleSymmetrization.lean#L73-L75","doc":"`2·e₂ = p₁² − p₂` — the antisymmetric (Pauli) sector. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Hecke.IdenticalParticle.two_h2","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Hecke.IdenticalParticle.two_h2","kind":"theorem","line":68,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Hecke/IdenticalParticleSymmetrization.lean#L68-L71","doc":"The two `S₂` sectors via the power sums `p₁ = a+b`, `p₂ = a²+b²`\n(no division): `2·h₂ = p₁² + p₂`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Hecke.IdenticalParticle.schur_weyl_Z2","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Hecke.IdenticalParticle.schur_weyl_Z2","kind":"theorem","line":60,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Hecke/IdenticalParticleSymmetrization.lean#L60-L66","doc":"**Z = 2 Schur–Weyl** (⁴He pp / nn). The naive distinguishable square\n`p₁² = (a+b)²` splits into the `S₂` symmetric sector `s₍₂₎ = h₂ = a²+ab+b²`\n(multiplicity `f^(2)=1`) and the antisymmetric sector `s₍₁,₁₎ = e₂ = ab`\n(`f^(1,1)=1`). An identical proton/neutron pair occupies one sector — the\ndoubly-magic projection the plain `^Z` power discards. ","signatureChunk":0}]}